#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# chop

#% text
# `chop(x)` replaces inexact numbers in `x` that are smaller than 10^-10 in absolute value by exact 0.

#% text
# `chop(x, delta)` uses the threshold `delta` instead of 10^-10.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Only inexact numbers, machine reals and balls, are replaced; exact numbers are never changed.

#% item
# A number is replaced when its absolute value is strictly less than the threshold.

#% item
# The real and imaginary parts of a complex number are chopped separately, so a tiny imaginary part disappears.

#% item
# `chop` works through any structure: lists, sums and other expressions.

#% item
# A ball is replaced when every number inside it is below the threshold, including a ball around 0.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
chop(1.0e-12)
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# Numbers above the threshold are kept:

#% cell
chop(0.5)
#% output
#= 0.5

#% text
# A tiny imaginary part from rounding:

#% cell
exp(pi*1.0*im)
#% output
#= -1.0 + 1.2246467991473532e-16im

#% cell
chop(exp(pi*1.0*im))
#% output
#= -1.0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
chop([1e-11, 1e-9, 2.0])
#% output
#= [0, 1e-9, 2.0]

#% text
# Inside a sum:

#% cell
chop(x + 1e-12)
#% output
#= x

#% text
# A ball around 0:

#% cell
chop(n(sqrt(2), 20) - n(sqrt(2), 20))
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A larger threshold:

#% cell
chop(1e-5, 1e-3)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Removing the rounding error of a machine computation that should give 0:

#% cell
sin(n(pi))
#% output
#= 1.2246467991473532e-16

#% cell
chop(sin(n(pi)))
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Exact numbers are never chopped, however small:

#% cell
chop(1/10^20)
#% output
#= 1/100000000000000000000

#% text
# A number equal to the threshold is kept:

#% cell
chop(1e-10)
#% output
#= 1e-10

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n`, `rationalize`, `round`, `precision`
